# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# KAYAN ERP - the settings a packaged desktop copy runs with.
#
# The desktop program does NOT read this file. It creates its own copy on the
# machine it is installed on, at:
#
#     %APPDATA%\KAYAN-ERP\kayan.env          (Windows)
#     ~/.local/share/KAYAN-ERP/kayan.env     (Linux)
#     ~/Library/Application Support/KAYAN-ERP/kayan.env   (macOS)
#
# with fresh random signing secrets, and reads that copy at every start. This
# file is only the reference: it says which settings exist and what they mean.
# It is safe to commit because it carries no real secret.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The API binds to the loopback address for a desktop installation. The shell
# passes this in; it is written here so the setting is documented.
HOST=127.0.0.1
PORT=3000
NODE_ENV=production

# The account the program uses to read and write the company's data.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://erp_app:postgres@127.0.0.1:5432/erp_kayan?schema=public"

# An administrator account, used once per installation to create the database
# and the account above if they are missing. Leave it empty to skip that step
# on a machine where the database is already prepared.
ADMIN_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres:postgres@127.0.0.1:5432/postgres"

# Signing keys for the tokens users sign in with. The desktop program writes
# long random values here on first run. Never copy a development value.
JWT_ACCESS_SECRET=""
JWT_REFRESH_SECRET=""
JWT_ACCESS_TTL=900
JWT_REFRESH_TTL=1209600

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Serving other desks (a company network) or the internet
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A desktop copy binds to 127.0.0.1 and is reachable only from its own machine.
# A company server binds to every interface so the other desks can connect:
#
#     HOST=0.0.0.0
#
# and every browser opening http://<address>:3000 that is not `localhost` is
# NOT a secure context. The browser refuses to store the session there, so the
# program keeps it in memory for that page and says so on screen. Sign-in works;
# a reload signs the person out. Serve the program over HTTPS to keep sessions.
#
# Behind an HTTPS reverse proxy (recommended - see docs/REMOTE_ACCESS.md):
#
#     KAYAN_TRUST_PROXY="loopback"      # the proxy runs on this machine
#     KAYAN_TLS_TERMINATED=1            # the proxy terminates HTTPS
#
# KAYAN_TRUST_PROXY makes Express read X-Forwarded-For / X-Forwarded-Proto, so
# audit rows, rate limits and CORS see the real caller instead of the proxy.
# Nothing is trusted when the variable is absent.
#
# Deliberately publishing this API directly on the internet is refused unless
# TLS is known to be in front of it:
#
#     KAYAN_ALLOW_PUBLIC_HTTP=1         # acknowledge the exposure
#
# with both variables above set, the server starts; with the exposure flag alone
# it refuses to start and prints what is missing. Do not open PostgreSQL (5432)
# to the network under any of these settings: the API is the only door.
#
# Sessions: JWT_ACCESS_TTL=900 (15 min) and JWT_REFRESH_TTL=1209600 (14 days)
# are the values the desktop shell uses. A longer access token weakens the
# protection that a stolen token stops working; a shorter one makes a point of
# sale re-authenticate in the middle of a sale. The client renews silently, so
# 15 minutes is enough - do not raise it to hide a networking fault.
